    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe refactor is largely well done \u2014 extracting `OwnerEmails` as the single source of truth for the disposable blocklist, routing `NormalizeOwnerEmail` through `isDisposable`, and placing `riskFlag` as a `default` method alongside the other derived mappers (`ownerSegment`, `ageBand`, etc.) with fully-qualified calls into `rest.function.owner` all match the established conventions in this file. One structural loose end, though:\n\n**`NormalizeOwnerEmail` still parses the domain inline instead of reusing the new `OwnerEmails.domainOf`.**\n- `OwnerEmails` is introduced in this very diff and its Javadoc declares it \"the single source of truth\" for email helpers, including domain handling (`domainOf`).\n- Yet `NormalizeOwnerEmail:32-33` keeps its own extraction: `lowered.substring(lowered.indexOf('@') + 1)`. So after the change there are two domain-parsing code paths in the same package \u2014 the helper the refactor just created, and the one it left behind in the file it was editing.\n- `riskFlag` correctly goes through `OwnerEmails.domainOf(owner.getEmail())`; `NormalizeOwnerEmail` should do the same (compute `domain` via `OwnerEmails.domainOf(trimmed)` / the lowered value) so the one place that defines \"the domain of an email\" is actually the one place. This keeps the new helper from immediately becoming a partial source of truth.\n\nMinor (not blocking on its own): `riskFlag` re-inlines the `Boolean.TRUE.equals(getPossibleDuplicate())` / `getCapacityWarning()` checks that are also used in the two `@Mapping` expressions just above it. That mirrors how `ownerSegment` reuses `locality(owner)`, so it's defensible, but the duplicated null-safe idiom is worth a glance while addressing the above.",
